    "title": "The time between booking a venue tour and arriving deserves a plan",
    "core_perspective": "When a couple schedules a tour a few weeks away, there's an opportunity to help them picture their wedding before they arrive. We look at what happens during that wait, including the emails they receive and where a personal conversation would help."
  },
  "sharpened_point": null,
  "strategic_purpose": {
    "pillar": "CONVERT",
    "content_type": "solution_selling",
    "why_now": "Make the value of pre-consult nurture concrete for venue owners already investing in visibility. This scene shows how we think about automated follow-up alongside the owner's personal sales conversations, giving us an earned connection to LeadLock and The Foolproof Follow-Up Advantage."
  },
  "audience": {
    "who": "Venue owners investing in getting their venue in front of the right couples and looking to carry that interest into tours and sales conversations.",
    "reason_to_care": "Once a couple has scheduled a tour, an owner may naturally focus on confirmations and reminders. This piece adds a useful question: what could help that couple picture their wedding and become more familiar with the venue while they wait?"
  },
  "intended_shift": {
    "understand": "The period before a venue tour can have a purpose beyond communicating the appointment details.",
    "believe": "Automated nurture and an owner's personal conversation can support each other. Useful pre-tour communication should reflect what the couple needs and what the owner can realistically offer.",
    "do": "Consider what couples receive before their tour, then inquire about the follow-up system WIA builds in LeadLock if they want support with that communication."
  },
  "point_of_view": "we",
  "wia_perspective": "During an earlier venue conversation, we discussed what couples received between scheduling a tour and arriving for it. The owner was already reaching out to offer an earlier visit, and some couples were coordinating their plans around family availability. Whitney asked about the venue's existing virtual-tour option and suggested an earlier phone or virtual connection. The team also proposed a supplemental email with wedding footage and an invitation to ask questions for couples with a longer wait.\n\nWhat interests us is the purpose of that communication. A reminder helps a couple keep track of their appointment. Wedding footage could help them picture the venue in use, and a personal conversation could give them a chance to ask questions before they arrive. We would consider those opportunities alongside the owner's schedule.\n\nThat is how we think about the automated emails and texts we build through The Foolproof Follow-Up Advantage in LeadLock. They support the owner's follow-up and sales conversations. The owner still leads the relationship.",
  "supporting_example": {
    "scene": "In this historical call, the venue owner described couples choosing later tour dates so family could attend, alongside limits on when the venue could host tours. She was already reaching out to see whether an earlier visit could work. The team proposed examining the language and personalization of the inquiry-to-tour nurture, while acknowledging that the existing email and text cadence was good.",
    "wia_move": "Whitney asked whether virtual tours were already available, then suggested using an earlier phone or virtual connection ahead of the later in-person visit. Fallon Looney, who is now former staff, proposed a supplemental email for couples with a longer wait, containing wedding footage and an invitation to ask questions. Public copy should attribute these separately to Whitney and the team, within an earlier conversation.",
    "client_contribution": "The owner supplied the scheduling context, explained her existing outreach and confirmed that virtual tours were already an option. WIA's recommendations responded to that information. Neither recommendation is documented as implemented."
  },
